One for 1sy thing: &nbsp;Pls fwd. me the GF email below without the additional chain below. These need to go in the SRA pack. &nbsp;TY&nbsp;&#8203;&#8203;&#8203;&#8203;Raminder&nbsp;GrewalGlobal&nbsp;HR&nbsp;Director+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;718From: Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Date: Monday, 8 September 2025 at 11:25To: Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: FW: NOx Department Hey R,Can you pls discuss with Trev before we respond to this. My email crossed with hers too. ThanksA&nbsp;​​​​Alicia&nbsp;AliniaInterim&nbsp;Chief&nbsp;Executive&nbsp;Officer+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;629From: Georgina Fallows &lt;gfallows@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 08 September 2025 11:23To: Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Priyanka Gladson &lt;pgladson@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Melissa Ferrari &lt;mferrari@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matthew Newbould &lt;mnewbould@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matt Hunt &lt;mhunt@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: NOx Department &nbsp;Dear Joseph, Howard, Alicia,&nbsp;&nbsp;Further to the below, and Michael’s email suggesting we speak on Monday, we write in advance of our next meeting.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Our interpretation of Friday’s meeting with Richard Coleman KC (“RCKC”) was not that set out in your email of 7 September 2025. It was repeatedly made clear that RCKC was advising the firm, not any of us as individuals, and that we have individual responsibility to ensure regulatory compliance. Whilst we appreciate that your email is intended to allay the concerns set out by us and other of the senior employees, we do not consider that has been achieved. We are also sceptical that Gramercy will agree to waive these clauses, given their continued insistence that those clauses feature and/or remain. &nbsp;&nbsp;We therefore write to inform you that Friday's meeting compounded rather than alleviated our concerns that the Credit Agreement ('G3') risks putting us in breach of our regulatory duties. While RCKC stated that it is in our clients’ best interests to receive funding, he also accepted that it is not in our clients’ best interests to perform a number of the obligations set out in G3 (the clauses discussed during Friday’s meeting, and identified in the letters attached to Céline Barnwell’s email of 5 September at 18:07 and Michael Burke’s email of 3 September 2025 09:28,including but not limited to clauses 5.12 b) and 5.49 a), c) and d). The Board’s solution to these problematic clauses was presented to those on the call as a “wait and see” approach, with attempted assurance that Gramercy would not require their performance, or in the event Gramercy did, that the Board would deal with it. The suggestion, in Joseph’s email, that Gramercy will “revisit these terms in a manner indicative of good faith and renewed confidence in the firm” is vague, and to the extent that it is reliant on “confidence in the firm” is severely undermined by the following.&nbsp;&nbsp;Of equal concern, we have not yet had an opportunity to address other serious issues, including: (i) the independence of the Board; (ii) the overall control exercised by Gramercy over this firm and its cases and; (iii) the execution of G3 including incorporating several clauses that were categorically flagged to the Board as being problematic from a regulatory perspective (strongly indicative of poor governance). In the unlikely event that Gramercy promptly removes all of the problematic clauses from G3, these three serious concerns remain entirely unaddressed. We are disappointed that the Board appears to view the only concerns as relating to the performance of G3, when the wider context of these three issues is far more troubling.&nbsp;&nbsp;Friday's discussion gave one illustration of these concerns relating to the execution of G3. In June this year, Alicia was sent counsel’s advice advising strongly against attempting to enter Gramercy into any NOx confidentiality ring. This opinion supported the strong opinion of all of the NOx cases leads. This advice was never shown to RCKC, who was instructed to advise the firm on G3. We presume this advice was however shown to Gramercy in advance of the execution of G3 (please correct us if that is inaccurate), yet, notwithstanding that advice, G3 was executed with clause 5.12 b) included. &nbsp;&nbsp;A further, even more serious example is that, following the email from Céline Barnwell on Friday at 18:07, it is clear from the attachments to that email that the board was sent multiple letters from multiple senior partners raising concerns with G3 (and a host of related regulatory issues), including the specific clauses discussed during the meeting with RCKC. Apparently in spite of these letters – which we understand have never been properly replied to – G3 was nevertheless executed.&nbsp;&nbsp;We therefore cannot accept the suggestion that a “new era” of governance began on 4 August 2025. On the contrary, the record demonstrates continuity with past practice, as confirmed by Alicia who stated during Friday’s meeting that she had been “intimately involved” in the negotiation of G3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;These are but two examples, illustrating why our concerns remain live, despite comments from the Board that any problematic clauses are a legacy of the “old regime” which, should Gramercy seek to require execution, the new Board will resist; and that Gramercy is apparently revisiting. The concerns around the independence of the Board and overall control exercised by Gramercy over the firm are set out in the letters to the Board from the senior partners dated 11, 14 and 22 August 2025 to which we have seen no satisfactory response. We therefore do not understand how Gramercy has “renewed confidence in the firm”, when it appears clear that the firm’s approach to governance remains unchanged.&nbsp;&nbsp;RCKC’s position appears to be that while he accepts some of the clauses may be problematic (notably 5.49 c) which contains representations that have been made, and possibly are continuing to be made, on behalf of the firm, as being in our client’s best interests, he considered concerns to be (a) hypothetical with a low likelihood of arising; and (b) curable by a protocol. However: &nbsp;&nbsp;(a) This advice, as we understand it and in line with RCKC’s comments at the meeting, is entirely bereft of any comprehension of &nbsp;Gramercy’s past behaviour, which we are not convinced RCKC has been fully made aware of or appreciated. The inclusion of clause 5.12(b) and the fact that this clause has been included and accepted by the Board prior to execution (and under the ‘new regime’) causes us serious concern. &nbsp;Note that for the avoidance of doubt, we understand RCKC to have been instructed on the construct of the actual clauses, as opposed to the performance of such clauses; &nbsp;(b)&nbsp;&nbsp; It ignores the Board’s lack of understanding, or in the alternative, highlights the lack of due diligence (which appears to have been intentional given comments by Howard and Joseph that they simply relied upon the previous regime’s negotiations and representations) when executing G3, something which is particularly troubling given G3’s importance and the fact that a member of the “old regime” continues to sit on the new Board. It is difficult for us to accept that such reliance has been given on an individual’s prior assessment of the inclusions of clauses in circumstances where the same individual was then suspended by the Board and remains (we assume) under investigation. This certainly was the case at the time we understand G3 was signed.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(c)&nbsp; The suggestion that professional obligations can/will be continually balanced against the need for funding, and “cured” by some form of protocol (which we note only applies to 5.12 (a)), keeps us in the same untenable position.&nbsp;&nbsp;10. Taken together, these issues solidify the risk of necessary and ongoing distraction from trial preparation. &nbsp;11. Of central concern is the sheer number of provisions we believe we cannot lawfully comply with, but which remain in an executed agreement. This puts us, and the company, at significant risk of being forced into an impossible choice in relation to various clauses of G3: &nbsp;Perform those obligations under G3 (for fear of reprisal that funding will be withdrawn due to a default of a clause of the agreement) and be in breach of our regulatory duties; or&nbsp;&nbsp;Decline to perform those obligations under G3 (because compliance would breach our regulatory duties), thereby jeopardising funding for our clients.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;12. All of the above makes the questions in our original email of 4 September 2025, 09:19, even more important, and our positions even more tenuous. &nbsp;13. There remains also our concerns and questions about the independence, structure and governance of the Board, as set out at paragraph 4(i) above and in the various letters written both by Defendants and PG employees, including Slaughter and May’s letter dated 13 August 2025 and letters dated 14 and 22 August 2025 attached to Céline Barnwell’s email of 5 September at 18:07. &nbsp;&nbsp;Please let us know of your earliest availability to discuss these issues.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Best regards,&nbsp;Michael Burke, Priyanka Gladson, Melissa Ferrari, Matt Newbold, Matt Hunt and Georgina Fallows&nbsp;(NOx case leads)​​​​Georgina&nbsp;FallowsLegal&nbsp;Director&nbsp;"}

