Joe,
&nbsp;
Thank you for sending this email and bringing this matter to our attention. It is very unfortunate that Tom Goodhead kept the firm leadership in the dark about the G3 facility. As Alicia and others know, we spent
 considerable time discussing the G3 credit facility with Tom and made every effort to close it prior to the end of June.
&nbsp;
As you may know, certain provisions within the G3 credit agreement were flagged by Tom in June as “regulatory points”. These provisions were subsequently negotiated directly between our regulatory counsel, Juliet
 Oliver from Kingsley Napley (who just recently left her position as Deputy Chief Executive and General Counsel at the SRA for over a decade), and PG’s regulatory counsel, Weightmans. After several weeks of negotiations, both PG and Gramercy agreed on the precise
 language for all of these “regulatory points”. In fact, on Tuesday, July 29, PG’s transaction counsel, McDermott, sent the attached light redline of the Credit Agreement (which does not have any changes to the “regulatory points”) to our counsel and Tom let
 us know that he was prepared to sign the attached July 29 draft Credit Agreement and close the facility as soon as possible. Unfortunately, Tom did not provide an operating budget for the use of proceeds under the G3 facility. This was the last open item for
 Gramercy to obtain our necessary approvals to close and we had been seeking the budget from PG for several months. Nick and Josh even went to London during the week of July 21 to meet with Tom personally to discuss the G3 operating budget but he did not agree
 to meet them in person. Instead, he dialed in by Zoom for a short time from the Netherlands and left the budget unresolved.
&nbsp;
As a result of the careful process and advice that both PG and Gramercy received from highly capable regulatory lawyers, Gramercy is comfortable that the G3 credit facility does not violate any applicable laws
 or disciplinary rules or place PG attorneys at risk. Notwithstanding the foregoing, Gramercy takes the concerns of the PG attorneys very seriously and would like to help build goodwill. Accordingly, Gramercy is willing to agree to the following with respect
 to the provisions that you cite below:
&nbsp;

Section 4.51 – Gramercy is prepared to waive our rights regarding the obligation for PG to appoint a Key Person to the board of each Stichting. Accordingly, PG
 is free to appoint directors in accordance with the internal governing documents of the Stichtings.
&nbsp;

Section 5.12 – Gramercy is prepared to accept PG’s proposal and has no desire to be allowed inside a Confidentiality Ring.
&nbsp;

Section 5.15 – Gramercy believes that the access to the case management system is already subject to Disciplinary Rules but please clarify if you believe differently.
&nbsp;

Section 5.43 – Gramercy is prepared to work together with PG to find alternative arrangements.
&nbsp;

Section 5.49 – Gramercy is prepared to waive our rights regarding the representation. Accordingly, PG is not in any way constrained or obligated based on this
 representation.
&nbsp;

Section 6.17 – Gramercy is prepared to accept PG’s proposal and make clear that regulatory obligations trump any such consents.
&nbsp;

Section 8.22 and 8.24 – As you know, these provisions address a handful of obligations in the Credit Agreement. Gramercy is prepared to agree that all PG obligations
 under 5.12(b), 5.49(a), 5.49(b) and 5.49(d) are subject to PG’s determination on what it considers to be its own regulatory obligations even if it conflicts with the Qualified Independent Legal Ethics Counsel.
&nbsp;
Finally, we are willing to ask our regulatory counsel, Juliet Oliver, to make herself available for any further questions that you or PG’s lawyers have with respect to the G3 credit agreement.
&nbsp;
Please do let us know if you have any questions. Regards,

Marc
