########################




Hi Nichola,&nbsp;




Following the completion of the trial (huzzah!) see below update to send to the Ford NLCFs.







########################




Dear colleagues,&nbsp;




We hope you are well. As you will be aware, Ford sat in trial from Monday 10 November until lunch on Tuesday 18 November. The Lead Solicitors set out below an update on our view of how the trial went.&nbsp;




Executive Summary




Overall, stepping back from the trial and looking at the evidence across the piste including that of Dr Davies, and the four experts, I think it is safe to say that the Claimant team did a magnificent job in presenting such a comprehensive and thorough case
 to Ford. The team of lawyers (both barristers and solicitors) who have worked night and day on this case for the last 3 years have done a remarkable job in turning Ford into such a strong case.&nbsp;




Looking at the key examinations of MR Carder (Claimant mechanical expert) and Professor Millo (Defendant mechanical expert), I think it is safe to say we are considerably more happy with how Mr Carder pformed than the Defendants would be regarding Professor
 Millo. Our counsel team were able to obtain significant admissions from Prof Millo that his report was not &quot;intended to be misleading&quot; as he corrected and clarified the report when in the witness box, while Mr carder was not put under such intense scrutiny
 whe non the stand. Of course, the Defendants may take a different view of things, but this is our perception of how the trial went for Ford.&nbsp;




As previously advised, Lady Justice Cockill is a very difficult judge to read, however it appears that she took significant interest in the case that we put before her and we remain hopeful that her judgment will fall positively for our case.&nbsp;




Looking forward, there is a further 3-4 week window for closing submissions about legal arguments listed for March 2026 across each of the lead cases. Closing submissions for that hearing are due to be submitted on 30 January 2026. Work for these has started
 already in respect of Ford and Mercedes. Peugeot Citroën, Renault and Nissan are still due to be heard (Peugeot is currently being heard in trial) and the closing submissions will address all 5 of the lead manufacturers.&nbsp;




Day one and two&nbsp;




We opened with the Ben Jaffey KC and Tom de la Mare KC on behalf of the Claimants' cross examining Dr Marcus Davies, a calibration manager who has worked
 at Ford for the past 25+ years.&nbsp;




Mr Jaffey took Mr Davies through his career at Ford before putting documents to him about how Ford calibrated their vehicles, including their deployment
 of the various Engine Control Systems (&quot;ECS&quot;) including EGR, LNT and SCR. He also put various points to Dr Davies on what &quot;reduction in effectiveness&quot; actually means in real world.&nbsp;




Dr Davies' evidence showed that:

1) Ford knew what had to be done to their vehicles to
 pass the regulatory test, the New European Driving Cycle (&quot;NEDC&quot;); and

2) Ford programmed or calibrated their vehicles to do that and little else more.




Dr Davies did not dispute this and in fact, agreed with it. When he was asked to explain why Ford later updated their vehicles to be able to perform to
 a standard that was better than just the narrow confines of the NEDC, and without changing any of the hardware in the vehicles, his answers were less than convincing. There was a dichotomy in his evidence: he suggested that Ford took years to develop engines,
 with extensive testing to establish the limits of the components to which the vehicles were calibrated but then Ford was able to update and expand the calibration of the vehicles, without changing any hardware. For example, Dr Davies was taken to documents
 showing that as far back as 2012, Ford was working to improve its EGR rates, but there was limited reason as to why they didn't implement this prior to selling the vehicles.&nbsp;




Tuesday morning focus moved to two further key areas: the 6th gear issue in the Transit van, and DeSOx:

1) Dr Davies was taken to documents that shows that certain Ford Transits, when using 6th gear, as indicated by the Gear Shift Indicator, increase their
 output NOx level by over 130%. Dr Davies did not seem surprised by this.&nbsp;

2) Similarly, he seemed unbothered by the DeSOx documents that were put to him. DeSOx is the terminology used to describe an issue which affects certain
 Ford vehicles with an LNT whereby the LNT catalyst becomes saturated with SOx (another noxious gas) such that it cannot effectively act to reduce NOx. Once the LNT is compromised by SOx, the vehicle spews out NOx at vast rates and so they must purge the LNT
 of SOx, by performing what are known as &quot;DeSOx events&quot; to cleanse the catalyst. This comes at a significant cost of fuel economy for which consumers have to pay. The Claimants' application to amend the pleadings remains outstanding, and this is due to be heard
 next year.




The Claimant team consider that nothing in Dr Davies' evidence immediately helped Ford's position. In certain respects, it assisted our case, including
 as regards updates and proof that Ford could have improved their vehicles earlier; instead Ford primarily focused on other issues such as cost and fuel economy as opposed to regulatory compliance and environmental issues.&nbsp;




On day two, the Claimant software expert, Dr Markus Heitz then took the stand. As with the September hearing, the Defendants sought to criticise Dr Heitz's
 report that it duplicated work in the Mechanical expert report, and that it was beyond his remit of instruction. Dr Heitz performed well under cross examination. Ultimately, however, much of the software analysis was agreed between the experts and Dr Heitz's
 cross-examination was quite short.&nbsp;




Day three and four




Day three opened with the Defendants cross examination of the Claimants' mechanical expert, Mr Dan Carder. Despite our reservations about Mr Carder, he equipped himself well over the course of the cross examination.&nbsp;




The Defendants' counsel team, consisting of Neil Moody KC and Sonia Nolten KC, took turns to cross examine Mr Carder, with Ms Nolten cross examining him on SCR and other available alternative technology, while Mr Moody covered &quot;everything else&quot;. The Defendants'
 cross examination seemed relatively light touch and they did not go into depth on much of Mr Carder's report.&nbsp;




Mr Carder dealt with their questions well and enjoyed good humour with the Judge, Lady Justice Cockerill.&nbsp; He came across genuine and authentic (which has been our experience of him) and the Defendants failed to probe Mr Carder on a number of tricky areas arising
 out of his report.&nbsp;




Mr Moody took Mr Carder through general points of principle and his analysis of the testing results. Mr Carder rightly pointed out that he was only there to present the data and make general points about what the Defendants could have done, at one point observing
 that it was not for him to &quot;design a new and compliant Ford vehicle for them&quot;.&nbsp; There were some comments made by Mr Carder that were not ideal, including his stating that &quot;less EGR does not necessarily mean more NOx&quot; but this was anticipated by us as being
 an opinion that he would share and so we were unsurprised.&nbsp;




Ms Nolten then took Mr Carder through the SCR technology, an area that the Claimants' case is not as strong on as other points. She made a number of observations and suggestions that Mr Carder agreed with which may be less helpful as we move into closing submissions.
 He ultimately did not agree with her suggestion that the modulation that Ford deployed was suitable&nbsp; - a key point in our argument that Ford did not calibrate their vehicles appropriately. Ms Nolten also cross-examined Mr Carder about alternative technologies.
 Mr Carder performed well.&nbsp;




The morning of day four had Ms Nolten finish her questioning on SCR and then move onto alternative technology, an area that Mr Carder performed well in. While he did not make any positive assertions&nbsp;about what alternative
 technology&nbsp;&quot;Ford should&quot;&nbsp;have been using in their vehicles, he was firm that &quot;Ford
could&quot;&nbsp;have been using alternative (and we say better) technology in their vehicles.
&nbsp;The Defendants' line of questioning on this at times seemed to suggest that Ford should not be held to the same standard as other manufacturers who were deploying better technology, and we think the Judge took note of this.
 Mr Carder asked the same question of Ms Nolten as to &quot;do you think that Ford couldn't do it if VW could&quot; who declined to answer the question.&nbsp;




He made a number of good points back to the Defendants in cross examination and, most importantly, the Defendants failed to interrogate him on a number of salient areas of his report which now means they go unchallenged and are accepted in evidence, which is
 important for the case.&nbsp;




Our junior barrister, Ms Joanna Buckley, lead the cross examination of Prof Migliorero, the Defendants' software expert. Similar to Dr Heitz, given much of the evidence was agreed, his cross examination was relatively short, however Ms Buckley managed to get
 a number of key admissions and agreements out of Prof&nbsp;Migliorero, which are salient for the overall case, and important for the cross examination of Ford's mechanical expert, Professor Frederico Millo, which commences on Monday.&nbsp;




It transpires that Prof Migliorero is a former student of Prof Millo and they have worked together closely over the years. Ms Buckley, in cross examination, put to Prof&nbsp;Migliorero that certain paragraphs in his and Prof&nbsp;Millo's reports were identical. Prof&nbsp;Migliorero
 confirmed that the wording was from his report, and it was in fact, incorrect, which we made for a mildly embarrassing position for Prof&nbsp;Millo.&nbsp;




Ms Buckley elicited from Prof&nbsp;Migliorero that software updates and the expansion of how the ECS operates were done within the constraints of the existing hardware but the main piece was that he confirmed the reports were prepared in concert.&nbsp;




Day four and five (half day)




Days four and five concentrated solely on the Defendants' mechanical engineering expert, Professor Millo. He was cross examined by Mr Jaffey KC.&nbsp;




Mr Jaffey took Prof Millo initially through his CV and relevant working experience (noting that he has worked for and with many other vehicle manufacturers over his career), before turning to his analysis of the rules surrounding the operation of the LNT. In
 what became a repetitive statement by Prof Millo, he had to apologise and explain to the Court that his report and the analysis contained within his report, was not intended to be misleading to the Court or to the parties. He repeated this statement five or
 six times during cross examination, also explaining to Mr Jaffey and the Court that his use of emotive and definitive language should be taken as stylistic (as that is how reports are written in Italian proceedings) and that there were numerous other ways
 in which his analysis could have been surmised. Our interpretation of how his evidence was delivered, was that Ford had not been entirely fair to Prof Millo when assisting him with his report, and that a delta existed between them that Mr Jaffey was successful
 in exploiting and putting on show to the Court.




Prof Millo also did not help himself by taking positions that were even more extreme than Ford, and disagreeing with results from other external investigations. When discussing conformity factors (i.e. the multiplier to the amount of NOx seen on the real world
 as opposed to the NEDC), Prof Millo took the position that the conformity factors that Ford considers to be reasonable, could have been widened. Similarly when discussing the testing results obtained by investigations by the Department for Transport and the
 French Royale Commission, Prof Millo instead of accepting the results were likely caused by the SOx issue, instead chose to say it was likely something else. Similarly he opted to criticise the driving route that the sample vehicles were put through despite
 having not looked at the route itself to analyse it. None of this endeared him to the Judge we believe.&nbsp;




Whilst there were a lot of points that Prof Millo agreed with Mr Jaffey's suggestions that his report did not fully represent the picture, i.e. becuase he had not been shown relevant documentation, or because he did not consider the entire suite of tests, all
 of which is incredibly helpful and supportive of the case we are advancing, one of the main admissions we got from Prof Millo came on Tuesday morning.&nbsp;




One of the main battlegrounds on Ford is the argument about what a &quot;reduction in effectiveness&quot; means, with Ford taking the position that just becuase one of the ECS in the vehicle is derated / modulated, this doesn't mean NOx goes up, and that you have to
 consider the ECS and all emissions (not just NOx) holistically. Our position, which is more narrow, is that ECS modulation that equates to NOx going up is a reduction in effectiveness. Ford have been careful in their position to not say that if for example
 the EGR is modulated, then NOx goes up, but that is precisely what Prof Millo said on Tuesday when he agreed with Mr Jaffey. Similarly, he also agreed with outside of the NEDC operating window, there is little to no EGR applied to the vehicle.&nbsp;




These are notable and significant points to get out of an expert and we were very pleased to have had Mr Jaffey get these admissions from him.




Overall, we believe Ford's time at Trial went well. Despite Ford having volunteered itself as an ALGLO back in December 2023, and having attempted to present itself as the one with clean hands through the process, the disclosure located by the teams and the
 expert reports paint a different picture. We are pleased with how teh trial went and we are already starting on the closing submissions for Ford, which are due on 30 January 2026 along with the other manufacturers.&nbsp;




We will be in touch with a further update in future.&nbsp;




Best wishes,




Michael &amp; Nichola






































