Hi all,



We should keep our response addressing the Defendants’ objections on an open&nbsp;basis. The open response should not refer to or hint at their WPSATC&nbsp;offer (which it currently does not, but lets double check). It will focus purely on the
 merits and proportionality of the costs claimed.



We need to decide whether our own offer should be made open or WPSATC.



If made WPSATC, it remains privileged until the Court assesses costs, but we can still rely on it at the summary assessment&nbsp;stage because the Court will consider any WPSATC offer when assessing
 conduct and reasonableness under CPR 44.2(4)(c).



If made open, it will be visible now and may assist in demonstrating reasonableness, particularly if we are confident that the figure is highly defensible and we would be content for the Court
 to see it immediately.



In practical terms, the distinction affects timing, not admissibility. The judge will see the offer in any event at the summary assessment. The question is tactical: do we want the figure visible only to the Defendants for now, or
 also to the Court as part of the open record?



The safest approach is to keep the rebuttal letter open&nbsp;and make the monetary offer WPSATC, unless we think the offer is so reasonable that it would help to anchor the Court’s view of our position.



Happy to discuss preferences.



Best,
