Hi all,&nbsp;I spoke with the brokers on Friday regarding the technical call we need to schedule with the case team for early this week. As you might expect, a portion of the meeting will now focus on the consequences of stepping down as lead solicitors. &nbsp;Below are the questions received from insurers since our update to them last week. &nbsp;@Neil Pigott, I’d be very grateful if you could consider how these additional points can be included as part of the talking points you prepared last week. &nbsp;Insurer Queries:1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Could you please provide us with an anticipated timeline for when the Court is expected to issue its order regarding PG’s position? Is it anticipated that the Court will order Leigh Day become the sole lead? Further, please can PG provide some commentary on the practical ramifications of them stepping down as lead solicitors? We note your comment that “The firm remains a fully active member of the Steering Committee and is in the process of agreeing mechanisms whereby it can continue work on the matter in substantive terms.”, which is helpful to understand. Could you please confirm what specific responsibilities PG handled in their capacity as lead, and the strategy for transferring these? Are there any unresolved tasks, specifically considering the proximity to trial?2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Who has authority to make a determination on settlement offers? Lead firm, Steering Committee, etc?3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Whether the stepping down causes PG to lose or vary any decision-making power for their own clients and the wider co-hort?Many thanks,Helena &nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Helena&nbsp;YearwoodRisk&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Compliance&nbsp;DirectorFrom: Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 26 September 2025 14:52To: Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Craig Shuttleworth &lt;cshuttleworth@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Neil Pigott &lt;neil.pigott@pallasllp.com&gt;; Plate Litigation &lt;plate_litigation@pallasllp.com&gt;Cc: Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jessica Fechner &lt;jfechner@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: FW: Update call - follow up queries&nbsp;PG team/Pallas &nbsp;See below query raised by insurers following yesterday’s communication: &nbsp;Could you please provide us with an anticipated timeline for when the Court is expected to issue its order regarding PG’s position? Is it anticipated that the Court will order Leigh Day become the sole lead? &nbsp;Further, please can PG provide some commentary on the practical ramifications of them stepping down as lead solicitors? We note your comment that “The firm remains a fully active member of the Steering Committee and is in the process of agreeing mechanisms whereby it can continue work on the matter in substantive terms.”, which is helpful to understand. Could you please confirm what specific responsibilities PG handled in their capacity as lead, and the strategy for transferring these? Are there any unresolved tasks, specifically considering the proximity to trial?&nbsp;I will acknowledge and liaise with WTW to ascertain what further questions may arise.&nbsp;Many thanks Helena &nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Helena&nbsp;YearwoodRisk&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Compliance&nbsp;DirectorFrom: James Burke &lt;James.Burke2@wtwco.com&gt; Sent: 26 September 2025 14:23To: Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;Cc: Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Dilan Alma &lt;dalma@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: RE: Update call - follow up queries&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Hi Helena,&nbsp;Thanks for the below. Insurers have passed us a few follow-up queries on the NOx update that we would be grateful for your assistance with.&nbsp;Could you please provide us with an anticipated timeline for when the Court is expected to issue its order regarding PG’s position? Is it anticipated that the Court will order Leigh Day become the sole lead?&nbsp;&nbsp;Further, please can PG provide some commentary on the practical ramifications of them stepping down as lead solicitors? We note your comment that “The firm remains a fully active member of the Steering Committee and is in the process of agreeing mechanisms whereby it can continue work on the matter in substantive terms.”, which is helpful to understand. Could you please confirm what specific responsibilities PG handled in their capacity as lead, and the strategy for transferring these? Are there any unresolved tasks, specifically considering the proximity to trial?&nbsp;&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;James Burke&nbsp;Associate Director (Solicitor)Litigation and Contingent Risk FINEX Global &nbsp;WTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116) On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai des Vennes, 4020, Liège, Belgium (registered as a branch in the UK at 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ UK Branch Number BR021056) in relation to all EEA-regulated business&nbsp;D: +44 (0)20 31248037M: +44 (0)7831 162591&nbsp;james.burke2@wtwco.com www.wtwco.com&nbsp;Financial, Professional, and Executive Risks (FINEX)Your Trusted Partner in Managing Complex Risk: Expertly navigating Litigation Risk, Cyber, Directors &amp; Officers, Financial Institutions, Mergers &amp; Acquisitions, Professional Indemnity, and Operational Risks within WTW. Part of our wider risk solutions capabilities at WTW&nbsp;At WTW we work flexibly - whilst you may receive emails from me out of working hours, I do not expect a response or action outside your own working hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;From: James Burke Sent: 26 September 2025 13:00To: 'Helena Yearwood' &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;Cc: Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Dilan Alma &lt;dalma@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: RE: Update call - follow up queries&nbsp;Many thanks Helena. We have updated insurers accordingly. &nbsp;Look forward to hearing from you on timings for the NOx call next week.&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;James Burke&nbsp;Associate Director (Solicitor)Litigation and Contingent Risk FINEX Global &nbsp;WTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116) On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai des Vennes, 4020, Liège, Belgium (registered as a branch in the UK at 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ UK Branch Number BR021056) in relation to all EEA-regulated business&nbsp;D: +44 (0)20 31248037M: +44 (0)7831 162591&nbsp;james.burke2@wtwco.com www.wtwco.com&nbsp;Financial, Professional, and Executive Risks (FINEX)Your Trusted Partner in Managing Complex Risk: Expertly navigating Litigation Risk, Cyber, Directors &amp; Officers, Financial Institutions, Mergers &amp; Acquisitions, Professional Indemnity, and Operational Risks within WTW. Part of our wider risk solutions capabilities at WTW&nbsp;At WTW we work flexibly - whilst you may receive emails from me out of working hours, I do not expect a response or action outside your own working hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;From: Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 25 September 2025 20:13To: James Burke &lt;James.Burke2@wtwco.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;Cc: Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Dilan Alma &lt;dalma@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: RE: Update call - follow up queries&nbsp;Hi James,&nbsp;Many thanks for your email. &nbsp;We acknowledge there has been a slight delay in sharing recent updates. &nbsp;&nbsp;Please find below latest developments regarding NOx and responses to Ignite’s queries. I’ll follow up shortly with a date for the meeting with the case leads, this is likely to be towards the middle of next week.&nbsp;&nbsp;Lead Solicitor&nbsp;The Firm has in consultation with the other Lead Solicitor and subject to the Court’s approval decided to step down temporarily as Lead Solicitor in the lead cases.&nbsp; This will obviate any attempt by the Defendants to distract the proceedings tactically by (unmerited) references to our personnel/legal team changes.&nbsp; The firm remains a fully active member of the Steering Committee and is in the process of agreeing mechanisms whereby it can continue work on the matter in substantive terms. We do not anticipate material financial impact on the conduct of the proceedings.&nbsp;&nbsp;Responses to Ignite’s queries: &nbsp;Key Personnel/Lead Lawyers - per the verbal request can the lead lawyers per NOx manufacturer and for the BHP matter be detailed to us please?&nbsp; The board of directors now comprises Alicia Alinia, Howard Morris, and Joseph Moreno of both the Parent and the Firm (the “Board”). Thomas Goodhead has now left both as CEO and Director of the Firm.&nbsp; The Board is responsible for the overall management of the Firm, and each member of the Board has statutory duties to exercise independent judgment in accordance with the Companies Act 2006. The Board appointments demonstrate that Pogust Goodhead is being run by an extremely experienced and committed team of independent, regulated, legal professionals. Each of the Board members is regulated by the SRA and qualified to practice in England &amp; Wales.&nbsp;Following Pete’s departure, Anna Varga, whom you have met, has taken the lead on Mercedes and Vauxhall cases and is overall supervisor for the remaining NOx cases. Working closely with our case leads, Anna will ensure a smooth transition and maintain consistency in how the cases are managed. For BHP, our case leads are Guy Robson and Faranak Ghajavand. Additionally, Craig Shuttleworth (Kastle Solicitors and previously Head of Litigation at Jones Day), who has decades of litigation experience is joining us as a strategic advisor. Craig has been working with the firm supporting BHP and other matters since 2023 and will work to support teams across both the NOx and BHP matters. We want to reassure you that the cases continue to receive the level of expertise and attention required. Our retained barristers and partner firms remain fully engaged and unchanged, providing continuity and stability in external representation. Internally, the team continues to operate with the senior oversight and leadership necessary to progress the cases effectively. We are confident that this structure offers the strongest platform for achieving a successful outcome.&nbsp;We would like to inform you that Michael Burke has formally tendered his resignation with Pogust Goodhead and is currently working his notice period. We will liaise with the Defendants in those Litigations to advise them accordingly. We will provide a more detailed response outlining further upcoming changes within the case teams, ensuring you have a clear understanding of the updated structure.&nbsp;Negotiating Partners - should they differ from 1, can the lawyers in charge of WP negotiations also be detailed to us please?These individuals are consistent with those mentioned above.&nbsp;Recent Costs Judgment - Last week a costs Judgment was published which saw the claimants budget reduced significantly for the trial. Does this cause any issues for PG and/or Gramercy? link: https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/kb/2025/2307The reductions that the court applied to the Quantum Trial budgets related mainly to costs submitted by non-lead claimant firms. The reductions applied to PG related costs were a relatively modest 30% after adjusting for non-lead claimant firm costs. This 30% reduction is modest both in the context of expected recoverable costs on success, and in the context of budget to adequately resource the case. Therefore, PG does not consider there to be any material effect on PG or Gramercy. Gramercy have also not raised any material concern.&nbsp;&nbsp;Happy to arrange a call to discuss, if helpful. &nbsp;Kind regards,Helena &nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Helena&nbsp;YearwoodRisk&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Compliance&nbsp;DirectorThis email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the P