act that there were a number of unsigned Priorities Deeds attaching to your ATE insurance policies, which we pointed out were an urgent priority to be signed. Please can we have an update on the status of these, and understand if there is a reason preventing these documents being signed? To date, we have not received any reason for the documents not being signed and some insurers have raised concerns, following all the recent press articles, as to what will happen, if the cases succeed.&nbsp;Below and attached we have collated the unsigned documents. There are 14 unsigned documents, dating back to August 2024. We have chased regularly and repeatedly since August 2024, but have not received any positive updates towards their signature. We note that for a number of documents the only signatories are PG and the insurers, and the insurers remain ready to sign.&nbsp;As we’ve highlighted to PG in our conversations, Clause 2(33) of the each of the insurance policies states that a Priorities Agreement to each of the policies must be signed within 30 days of the policy being incepted, which in each case is well overdue. Per clauses 5.3.2, 7.1(d) and 7.2 of each policy non-compliance by PG gives Insurers an ability to avoid payment of any claims made on the policy. Having these documents unsigned is a major risk to PG’s adverse cover and your ability to make confirmations about your adverse coverage to the Court, should your adverse coverage arrangements be scrutinised by the Court at any point. For these reasons, these documents need to be signed as soon as possible.&nbsp;Given the change of governance at PG, please can these documents now be signed? Should you wish to discuss with me directly, please let me know.&nbsp;Kind regardsJonathan&nbsp;Jonathan Simon LLB (Hons)&nbsp;International Head of Litigation and Contingent Risk FINEX GlobalWTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116)On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai d