Thanks for your email, Helena.
&nbsp;
For illustrative purposes, I have set out below, the practical steps and the costs for (as an example) a £4m ATE policy with Meridian.
&nbsp;
Cell Solution: practical steps
The ATE policy is provided by our captive insurance cell Meridian, but is collateralised by PG (or by your funder, Gramercy). Key points are therefore:

Collateralisation: this can be in any form that is to Meridian’s satisfaction. It is typically by way of
 letter of credit provided by PG/Gramercy’s bank, but could be in another suitable form e.g. deposit of cash into an escrow account with Meridian. PG would need to arrange the Letter of Credit with their banking partners, and the bank would need to be of suitable
 standing (e.g. at least A-/BBB rated or US Federal bank/similar). If the identity of the bank can be advised as soon as possible then the necessary checks can be made by Meridian. There may also be an associated cost to arranging the LoC, but it would be for
 PG’s bank to advise. Meridian documentation: two documents are required:

Meridian participation agreement with PG, with PG as beneficiary that sets out how the Meridian cell financials will work. Meridian can draw up a draft
 of this agreement for your review.Policy wording: bespoke wording can be used, but we would propose it mirrors the PG’s template emissions ATE wording. The policy would include an AAE.
KYC:
Meridian will need to perform the usual KYC checks on PG/Gramercy (as applicable) prior to entering into the arrangement. Checks on PG should be straightforward as WTW has conducted prior checks.Timing:
once the Letter of Credit is in place / KYC has been cleared, we/Meridian should be able to move quickly in order to get a policy in place. Meridian advise that following receipt of suitable policy collateralisation,
 the policy can be put in place in about a week. Below is a flow-chart giving the step-by-step process that the Meridian option would involve.
&nbsp;
Costs
The below costings are on the basis of coverage of a £4m policy. The fees to consider are:


Meridian fee – GBP100k for year one, followed by £30k per year thereafter (until the conclusion of proceedings)
Willis Brokerage @ 15%
UK Insurance Premium Tax @12%, if applicable.

Small surplus in case of operational costs
Using Meridian, we can reduce the upfront premium cost outlay to PG, compared to placing the risk via the traditional ATE market. So, by way of example,
 Meridian could look to charge a 10% Rate On Line for the Deposit Premium.
On a £4m Limit of Indemnity, the DP would be £400k. This would be paid to and held by Meridian, however investment interest would accrue on the cash which PG would be entitled to.

&nbsp;
The Deferred &amp; Contingent premium is, again, up to PG. Given there is very little capacity left in the ATE market for Emissions cases, a D&amp;C of up to 100% ROL could be argued as reasonable,
 although this could ultimately be reviewed by the Court/challenged by the Claimants.
&nbsp;
Using a flat 50% ROL is probably more advisable. If so, the cash Meridian would need would be as follows:
&nbsp;


Deposit Premium - £400k
Meridian fee – £100k
Willis Brokerage – GBP60K (15% of £400k DP)
Insurance Premium Tax £48K (12% of £400k DP), if applicable
Small surplus in case of operational costs – £20k. We think that this will be sufficient but in exceptional circumstances more operational funds may be required and can be billed for should it arise.
Total paid to Meridian: £628,000
(plus £30k yearly Meridian set-up fees)
&nbsp;
Letter of Credit required: £3,600,000 (i.e. £4m LOI minus £400,000 Deposit Premium). The policy is collateralised by the LOC, plus the Deposit Premium.
&nbsp;
The D&amp;C premium that PG is entitled to of £2m (subject to brokerage and IPT) &nbsp;would be recoverable via the waterfall from the damages received in the case. In effect, PG would be standing as insurer
 as they are collateralising the Meridian policy and therefore stand to receive a return through the D&amp;C premium on success. The D&amp;C amount can be stated to be inclusive or exclusive of brokerage and IPT, again depending on how PG wish to structure the policy
 terms.
&nbsp;
If the case is successful, the LoC will not be called in and PG will be returned the Deposit Premium and earn the Deferred &amp; Contingent premium (subject to brokerage and IPT, if applicable). If
 the case is unsuccessful, the entirety of the LoC and the deposit premium could be called upon to meet the adverse costs liability.
&nbsp;
Flow-chart
Please find below a flow chart giving the step-by-step process that the Meridian option would involve. With a fair wind, we/Meridian may be able to move faster
 if necessary than the times indicated, if that is what is required.
