t date when it was issued and b) when it was communicated to staff that it had been issued?&nbsp;If you could also provide me with the version of the policy that was in place immediately prior to this iteration of the policy that would be appreciated.&nbsp;Clearly if this iteration has been issued with changes to it, and it is said that this document forms part of an employee's contract, then any changes to it should be notified to the staff to whom it applies as it amounts to a change in contractual terms.&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;MichaelFrom: Natasha Geraldo Baird &lt;ngeraldobaird@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent: Thursday, December 4, 2025 10:13:27 PMTo: Michael Burke &lt;burke7y@hotmail.com&gt;Cc: HR Team &lt;hr@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: RE: M Burke &nbsp;Hello Michael,&nbsp;I have seen your email, and I am sharing a copy of the Sickness Absence Policy here for your reference.&nbsp;Please let me know if you need anything else or have any further questions.&nbsp;Kind regards,N &nbsp;​​​​Natasha&nbsp;Geraldo&nbsp;BairdHead&nbsp;of&nbsp;HR+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;643This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our&nbsp;website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our&nbsp;Privacy Policy.From: Michael Burke &lt;burke7y@hotmail.com&gt; Sent: 04 December 2025 21:07To: Natasha Geraldo Baird &lt;ngeraldobaird@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; HR Team &lt;hr@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: Fw: M Burke&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Dear Colleagues,&nbsp;Having received an out of office from Natasha advising she is out of office until Monday 8 December, and given the urgency to establish the position regarding the Sickness Policy and SSP, I am forwarding this email to the HR inbox.&nbsp;I look forward to hearing from you.&nbsp;Kind regards,Michael&nbsp;From:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;burke7y@hotmail.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Thursday, December 4, 2025 9:00 pmTo:&nbsp;Natasha Geraldo Baird &lt;ngeraldobaird@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: M Burke &nbsp;Dear Natasha,&nbsp;Thank you for your email and for providing responses to my queries.&nbsp;&nbsp;Firstly, thank you for confirming that my final pay will include payment for my six days accrued but untaken annual in addition to the pro-rated acting up allowance for the 15 days of employment with the firm in December.&nbsp;Thank you also for confirming the position of the company equipment, it is relieving to hear that as long as the courier collects this on the 12th, there will not be any withheld payment if they do not deliver it by the 15th. Please let me know the details of the courier so I can ensure the equipment is not in use and is ready to be collected.&nbsp;I will attend the office to return the remainder of my equipment (noting some of it remains in the flat in London along with some of my personal items so I need to be in London to deal with that in any event).&nbsp;&nbsp;With regard to the OSP v SSP, thank you for confirming the point regarding the Sickness Policy.&nbsp;&nbsp;I am afraid I do not think I have ever been provided with this. Can you please provide me with this?&nbsp;I also not the firm's position that the policy has been applied consistently. I reserve my position on this for now.&nbsp;Additionally, should I need to return to work, contrary to specific medical advice I.e. the medical document I have provided confirming that I should not be working, on account of being required to, to be eligible for full pay, then I also reserve my position on this.&nbsp;&nbsp;Thank you again for turning this around and for the confirmations provided.&nbsp;&nbsp;I look forward to hearing from you.&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;Michael&nbsp;Sent from Outlook for AndroidFrom:&nbsp;Natasha Geraldo Baird &lt;ngeraldobaird@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Thursday, December 4, 2025 8:20:27 PMTo:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;burke7y@hotmail.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: M Burke &nbsp;Dear Michael,&nbsp;Thank you for your email and for the welcome back. I appreciate you keeping us informed about your fitness for work, and I have answered your questions below.&nbsp;Eligibility for Occupational Sick Pay (OSP)Clause 13.4 of your employment contract states that company sick pay is provided “in accordance with the Company’s Sickness Policy.”&nbsp;The Sickness Policy, therefore, forms part of the contractual framework, and its eligibility criteria apply in full. Under the Policy, employees who have given notice of termination of their employment are not eligible for OSP.&nbsp;To address your question about consistency, I can confirm that the firm has consistently applied the current policy. Employees in comparable circumstances, i.e., those where notice has been given, receive SSP rather than OSP.&nbsp;Return to work and IT accessIf you become medically fit to return to work before 15 December, please let me know as soon as possible. In this instance, we would arrange for the IT team to reinstate your system access so that you can resume work and complete the handover required before your final day.&nbsp;Final payYour final pay will include:SSP for the period of certified sickness absencePayment for your six days of accrued but untaken annual leaveAny days on which you return to work and perform duties before 15 December, which will be paid at your normal rate.&nbsp;Regarding the acting-up allowance, this will be included in your final salary, pro-rated to your last day of employment, as you mentioned below.&nbsp;Company equipmentThank you for confirming that 12 December in the afternoon is suitable for the courier collection; I will arrange this accordingly.&nbsp;The temporary withholding of £1,000 is only to ensure the equipment is returned. Provided the courier collects the items on 12 December as arranged, the firm will not withhold the amount due to any delay caused by the courier’s delivery timeline.&nbsp;TELUS HealthI’m glad to hear that you intend to utilise TELUS Health services, and I encourage you to take full advantage of the support available.&nbsp;If you need any more clarification or if your fitness to return changes, please let me know, and we will support you accordingly.&nbsp;Kind regards,Natasha&nbsp;​​​​Natasha&nbsp;Geraldo&nbsp;BairdHead&nbsp;of&nbsp;HR+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;643This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at 53-64 Chancery House, Ch