Thank you for your email.&nbsp; I have also been provided with your emails to Alicia from today regarding a potential article in
The Times, suggestions of criminal conduct and concerns over Gramercy.&nbsp; This email responds to them&nbsp;together.
&nbsp;
As previously communicated to you, we will not be releasing you from your notice period.&nbsp; Your continued attempts to leave earlier than your contract allows are proving disruptive to our efforts to safeguard good governance
 within the firm.&nbsp;&nbsp;In addition, your conduct threatens to seriously damage morale and be more disruptive than helpful, taking account of the best interests of our clients.
&nbsp;
We are, therefore, minded to&nbsp;give you the option of being placed&nbsp;on&nbsp;paid leave for the remainder of your notice period.&nbsp; We would view that as regrettable since we are in the midst of the PDD Trial and would much prefer
 to have you remain&nbsp;working&nbsp;on the team.&nbsp;&nbsp;
&nbsp;
We therefore give you the choice of either to (i) stay working throughout the remainder of your notice period or (ii) go on&nbsp;paid&nbsp;leave effective immediately.
&nbsp;
If you continue to try to exit early,&nbsp;given the unparticularised nature of your allegations, we can only conclude that you are grasping for reasons to engineer such an exit for your own reasons, which is unprofessional,
 threatens breaches of your employment contract, and is not in the best interests of our clients.&nbsp; We put you on notice that we will not hesitate to take legal action against you to enforce your employment contract (in particular your notice period and all
 covenants), nor will we hesitate to take action against any firm that might seek to employ you ahead of your notice period expiring or in breach of your covenants.
&nbsp;
If you choose to remain&nbsp;working&nbsp;at the firm, we must insist that you focus solely on your work and only raise any further concerns with sufficient particularity and justification so that we can understand them fully
 and investigate them if necessary.&nbsp; Unclear and unparticularised allegations as you have been raising for the past several weeks are simply improper and must stop.
&nbsp;
Regarding the specific points raised in your emails:
